{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"136b0064\" lines=\"25-39\">\n<description>\nDraw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.\n- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.\n- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).\n- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.\n- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.\n\nKey insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1be83260\" lines=\"41-58\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.\n\nInput structure:\n- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.\n- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.\n- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.\n\nRule:\n- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.\n- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.\n- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.\n- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,\n  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"963c33f8\" lines=\"86-108\">\n<description>\nDrop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.\n- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows\n  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Erase the top marker.\n- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.\n- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the\n  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.\n- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;\n  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the\n  run one column right before bottom-aligning.\n- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear\n  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean\n  \"stick to the first obstacle,\" while maroon cells mean \"fall through.\"\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"97c75046\" lines=\"88-107\">\n<description>\nMove the single marker to the endpoint reached by sliding along the obstacle contour.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background color fills the canvas.\n- One single-pixel marker sits away from a connected obstacle region of the\n  remaining color.\n- The marker has exactly one horizontal or vertical sightline to the obstacle.\n\nRule:\n- Find the last background pixel before the obstacle along that sightline.\n- From that pixel, try the two diagonal turns that continue around the\n  obstacle boundary.\n- Move the marker to the endpoint of the turn that remains 4-adjacent to the\n  obstacle for the longest distance.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not placed by distance to the obstacle; it first points to the\nobstacle, then follows the visible outside contour until that contour segment ends.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"20270e3b\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nConnect two sprites using matching connector pixels, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background color `bg`, sprite color `obj`, connector color `ctrl` (small, 2 components).\n- Two main foreground sprites, plus possibly tiny detached pixels belonging to one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the **base** sprite (closest to grid top-left).\n- Translate the other sprite so its connector sits one cell above the base's connector.\n- Stamp the plug onto the base; remove connectors (recolor `ctrl` to `obj`).\n- Output is the tight crop around all non-background pixels.\n\nKey insight: The connector pixels define the unique docking point and direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"edb79dae\" lines=\"39-53\">\n<description>\nStamp recolored sprites into a framed region using placeholder positions and a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background, a rectangular single-color frame, colored placeholder cells inside the frame.\n- Outside the frame: sprite shapes (one per placeholder color) and a small legend mapping each placeholder color to an output color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the frame and its interior placeholders.\n- For each placeholder color, locate the matching-color sprite outside the frame.\n- Stamp each sprite at the placeholder's position inside the frame.\n- Recolor every stamped sprite to the output color defined by the legend.\n\nKey insight: The legend pairs (overlapping two-color glyphs outside the frame) dictate the final recoloring — placeholder color selects the shape, legend maps it to the output color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5adee1b2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nUse the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n- Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n- For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n- Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n\nKey insight:\n- The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"494ef9d7\" lines=\"16-30\">\n<description>\nSlide each blue/teal or yellow/orange row pair together while preserving its left-to-right order.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background.\n- Each nonempty row contains exactly two distinct foreground pixels.\n- Blue/teal and yellow/orange row pairs are the moving pairs; other color pairs are distractors.\n\nRule\n- For each moving pair, keep the left pixel fixed and move the right pixel left until it touches the left pixel.\n- Clear the moved pixel's original location, and leave non-moving rows unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
